WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.059 --> 00:00:16.240
question of your future came up at today's meeting," Mrs.

2
00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:17.000
Lippett repeated.

3
00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:20.361
"And we discussed it in great detail."

4
00:00:21.202 --> 00:00:24.943
I continued to sit in stunned silence in the matron's office.

5
00:00:24.963 --> 00:00:29.204
"You've done well here, Jerusha," she went on.

6
00:00:30.136 --> 00:00:32.357
"And we let you attend the village high school.

7
00:00:33.598 --> 00:00:35.940
Despite some problems with your behavior."

8
00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:37.921
She frowned at me.

9
00:00:39.322 --> 00:00:40.522
I tried to look guilty.

10
00:00:40.542 --> 00:00:44.965
"But you're 18 now and finishing high school.

11
00:00:45.906 --> 00:00:47.847
We can't take care of you for much longer.

12
00:00:49.148 --> 00:00:52.830
As you know, we don't often keep orphans after age 16.

13
00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:55.912
You were lucky to have two extra years here."

14
00:00:57.704 --> 00:01:02.087
I'd worked hard in exchange for my stay at the orphanage for those two extra years.

15
00:01:04.248 --> 00:01:06.749
And my education had always come second.

16
00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:10.131
Today, for example, Mrs.

17
00:01:10.191 --> 00:01:12.393
Lippett had kept me home from school to work.

18
00:01:15.054 --> 00:01:18.016
But I was too anxious about my future to say anything.

19
00:01:19.377 --> 00:01:21.338
"Usually we'd send you out to find a job.

20
00:01:22.438 --> 00:01:25.120
However, your grades have been excellent.

21
00:01:26.388 --> 00:01:29.770
Miss Pritchard, who's on our ladies' committee, talked to your English teacher.

22
00:01:31.631 --> 00:01:33.652
The teacher said your work was brilliant.

23
00:01:35.914 --> 00:01:39.936
She also gave Miss Pritchard a story you'd written called 'Blue Wednesday.'"

24
00:01:42.478 --> 00:01:44.919
This time my guilty expression was real.

25
00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:49.942
Mrs. Lippett always said my imagination would get me into trouble.

26
00:01:51.663 --> 00:01:53.184
And now, I guess, it had.

27
00:01:54.412 --> 00:01:56.313
"Blue Wednesday" was a work of fiction.

28
00:01:57.353 --> 00:02:01.495
But it was based on the days when the trustees and ladies' committee came to visit.

29
00:02:01.515 --> 00:02:04.736
"Miss Pritchard read that story aloud to us.

30
00:02:06.797 --> 00:02:08.878
I think it was rude to mock the orphanage.

31
00:02:10.459 --> 00:02:12.500
Especially after we've done so much for you."

32
00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:15.421
Mrs. Lippett looked at me with disapproval.

33
00:02:15.441 --> 00:02:21.343
"Luckily, some people have a strange sense of humor and found your story funny.

34
00:02:22.828 --> 00:02:27.231
Mr.-that is, the trustee who just left-thought that it was quite amusing.

35
00:02:27.791 --> 00:02:33.055
On the strength of that disrespectful paper, he's sending you to college!"

36
00:02:35.296 --> 00:02:36.257
"To college?"

37
00:02:37.038 --> 00:02:38.218
My eyes grew big.

38
00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:41.420
Mrs. Lippett nodded.

39
00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:45.243
"He waited until after the meeting to discuss the details with me.

40
00:02:46.444 --> 00:02:47.625
They are quite unusual.

41
00:02:48.485 --> 00:02:49.846
I must say the gentleman is odd.

42
00:02:51.127 --> 00:02:52.308
He believes you have talent.

43
00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:55.582
He plans to educate you to become a writer!"

44
00:02:55.602 --> 00:02:57.363
"A writer?"

45
00:02:57.924 --> 00:02:58.764
I said in shock.

46
00:02:58.785 --> 00:03:02.267
"Who knows if you'll succeed.

47
00:03:02.988 --> 00:03:04.069
Only time will tell."

48
00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:07.532
The matron described the trustee's plan.

49
00:03:08.593 --> 00:03:11.775
I would spend the rest of the summer working at the orphanage.

50
00:03:12.936 --> 00:03:15.358
Miss Pritchard would help me choose new clothes.

51
00:03:16.579 --> 00:03:17.760
The trustee would pay for them.

52
00:03:18.641 --> 00:03:22.324
He would also pay my tuition and board directly to the college.

53
00:03:23.188 --> 00:03:26.911
I would also receive an allowance of 35 dollars a month.

54
00:03:26.931 --> 00:03:32.434
"That's a very generous amount, Jerusha," said Mrs.

55
00:03:32.494 --> 00:03:37.617
Lippett. "Far too generous for a girl who has never managed her own money.

56
00:03:38.878 --> 00:03:41.780
But he wanted you to feel equal to the other girls at college."

57
00:03:43.181 --> 00:03:47.444
Mrs. Lippett explained that the trustee's secretary would send me the money every month.

58
00:03:49.544 --> 00:03:54.225
"In return for all this, you must write letters to the trustee," she said.

59
00:03:54.245 --> 00:03:57.926
"These letters should not just be thank-you notes!"

60
00:03:59.527 --> 00:04:01.107
"Then what should I write about?"

61
00:04:02.027 --> 00:04:04.188
This gentleman did sound very odd.

62
00:04:04.208 --> 00:04:08.709
"He wants you to write about your studies and your daily life.

63
00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:13.731
Write a letter like you'd write to your parents—if you had parents."

64
00:04:16.151 --> 00:04:18.912
Mrs. Lippett had more strange instructions for me.

65
00:04:23.443 --> 00:04:25.264
The gentleman's name was not Mr.

66
00:04:25.324 --> 00:04:28.686
John Smith, yet that was how I should address the letters.

67
00:04:29.746 --> 00:04:31.608
I should mail them to his secretary, Mr.

68
00:04:31.648 --> 00:04:33.649
Griggs. "Mr.

69
00:04:33.709 --> 00:04:35.590
Smith will never answer your letters.

70
00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:37.911
He hates writing letters!

71
00:04:39.232 --> 00:04:41.834
However, he wants to keep track of your progress.

72
00:04:42.374 --> 00:04:45.116
He thinks letter writing is good training for a future author.

73
00:04:46.156 --> 00:04:50.979
The letters are the only thanks he requires, so treat them like a bill that must be paid.

74
00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:56.683
If there is an emergency "An emergency?"

75
00:04:57.564 --> 00:05:00.005
I hadn't thought about having an emergency at college.

76
00:05:01.947 --> 00:05:04.949
"For example, if you are expelled Oh my!

77
00:05:05.749 --> 00:05:06.930
I hope that doesn't happen.

78
00:05:07.991 --> 00:05:10.192
What an embarrassment for the John Grier Home!"

79
00:05:11.613 --> 00:05:15.256
Mrs. Lippett paused for a moment to calm herself, and then began again.

80
00:05:16.992 --> 00:05:19.593
"If you have an emergency, you may write to Mr.

81
00:05:19.613 --> 00:05:22.755
Griggs." I looked at the door.

82
00:05:24.616 --> 00:05:28.197
My head was whirring with excitement, and I wanted to escape.

83
00:05:29.498 --> 00:05:30.238
I needed to think.

84
00:05:30.818 --> 00:05:31.359
I stood up.

85
00:05:31.879 --> 00:05:32.379
But Mrs.

86
00:05:32.419 --> 00:05:34.480
Lippett shook her head and I sat back down.

87
00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:39.102
"I hope that you're grateful for this wonderful opportunity.

88
00:05:40.083 --> 00:05:42.744
Not many orphans get a chance to rise in the world.

89
00:05:44.084 --> 00:05:47.246
I hope your behavior will make us proud at the John Grier Home.

90
00:05:48.026 --> 00:05:50.948
You must always remember—" "Yes, ma'am.

91
00:05:51.268 --> 00:05:52.009
Thank you, ma'am.

92
00:05:52.889 --> 00:05:55.131
I must sew a patch on one of the orphan's shirts.

93
00:05:55.451 --> 00:05:55.891
Right now."

94
00:05:57.492 --> 00:05:59.874
I leaped up and fled, leaving Mrs.

95
00:05:59.934 --> 00:06:02.135
Lippett standing there with her mouth open.
